    "result_text": "Confirmed \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class with `@Test` methods, and the full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the city-capacity rule for `POST /api/owners`: creating an owner is now rejected with **409 Conflict** when the target city already contains 50 or more owners.\n\nFollowing the existing `RequireUniqueHousehold`/`DuplicateHousehold` pattern in the codebase, I added:\n\n1. **`RequireCityCapacity.java`** (`rest/function/owner/`) \u2014 a pipeline step that counts existing owners with a matching city (exact match, consistent with `AssignCustomerCode`) via `ownerRepository.findAll()`, and throws `CityAtCapacityException` when the count is \u2265 50. It runs before `BuildOwner`, so no owner is persisted on conflict.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException.java`** (`rest/escalation/`) \u2014 a checked exception carrying the offending city.\n\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java`** (`rest/escalation/`) \u2014 responds `409 Conflict` with body `{\"errors\":[\"city\"]}`.\n\n4. **`...CityAtCapacityException.yml`** (`officefloor/escalation/`) \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `requireCityCapacity` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `requireUniqueHousehold` and `build`.\n\nThe target test `coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a city to 50, expect the 51st to 409) passes, and the full test suite is green.",
